faqs:
  - question: "Do I need to change my code to get the faster cache?"
    answer: "No. Queries that already pass a `ttl` to `listRows` get the smaller, faster cache entries. The change is inside Appwrite's cache layer, so the SDK calls and the API responses stay the same."
  - question: "How much less memory does a cached list response use?"
    answer: "A cached page of 25 rows is 37% smaller, a page of 100 rows is 50% smaller, and a page of 1,000 rows or more is 67% to 69% smaller. A cached page of 5,000 rows drops from 3.38 MB to 1.06 MB."
  - question: "How much faster are cached list queries?"
    answer: "In benchmarks on self-hosted Appwrite 2.3, a server handled 5% more cached requests per second for pages of 25 rows and 24% to 29% more for pages of 250 rows or more. A single request for a cached page of 1,000 rows took 32.6 ms instead of 42.0 ms."
  - question: "Why does the speedup stop growing at larger page sizes?"
    answer: "The binary format only speeds up the cache read. Appwrite still converts every response to JSON before it sends it to the client, and that work also grows with the page size. Cache memory has no such conversion step, so the memory saving keeps growing up to 69%."
  - question: "Do self-hosted instances need a migration to use the new format?"
    answer: "No. After the upgrade to Appwrite 2.3, Appwrite reads an old JSON cache entry as a cache miss, loads the data from the database, and writes the entry again in the binary format. Cache misses rise for a short time after the upgrade."
  - question: "Do requests without a TTL get faster too?"
    answer: "Yes, by a smaller margin. Appwrite also caches documents such as sessions, API keys, and table schemas in Redis, and all of them now use the binary format. Uncached requests in the benchmark were about 5% faster, which is smaller than the gain for TTL-cached lists."
---

The [TTL cache for list queries](/blog/post/announcing-list-cache-ttl) lets Appwrite answer a repeated `listRows` call from Redis instead of the database. On a cache hit, Appwrite reads the stored entry from Redis and converts it back into rows, then builds the response.

Appwrite now stores that cache in a binary format instead of JSON. Each cached page is smaller, and Appwrite reads it faster. In benchmarks, cached list queries used up to **69% less memory** and served up to **28% more requests per second**.

# The cache now uses a binary format instead of JSON

Appwrite used to write every cache entry to Redis as JSON. JSON is text, so each row in a cached page repeats every column name, and JSON stores every number as a string of digits. On a hit, Appwrite had to parse all of that text again.

The cache now uses [igbinary](https://github.com/igbinary/igbinary), a compact binary format for PHP data. igbinary stores a repeated string, such as a column name, only once per entry, and it stores numbers as numbers. Appwrite writes less data to Redis, reads less data back, and spends less CPU time on decoding.

Larger pages repeat the same column names more times, so they get the biggest saving.

# Cached pages use up to 69% less memory

![Cache size per cached page, JSON compared with binary](/images/blog/faster-list-caching-binary-format/cache-memory.avif)

The table shows the size of one cached page in Redis for each page size.

| Rows per page | JSON | Binary | Reduction |
|---|---|---|---|
| 25 | 16.3 KB | 10.3 KB | 37% |
| 100 | 66.6 KB | 33.6 KB | 50% |
| 250 | 167.8 KB | 64.4 KB | 62% |
| 1,000 | 672.8 KB | 218.9 KB | 67% |
| 5,000 | 3.38 MB | 1.06 MB | 69% |

The same Redis memory now holds about **three times** as many cached pages of 1,000 rows.

The saving applies to the whole cache. After a mixed workload of 200 cached queries, 500 single-row reads, and the table schemas, the full cache used **2.65 MB** instead of **5.66 MB**.

# Cached list queries serve up to 28% more requests

![Cached listRows requests per second for each page size](/images/blog/faster-list-caching-binary-format/throughput.avif)

One Appwrite server handled these cached `listRows` requests per second, with 8 requests in flight at a time.

| Rows per page | JSON | Binary | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| 25 | 508 | 533 | +5% |
| 50 | 445 | 485 | +9% |
| 100 | 350 | 398 | +14% |
| 250 | 215 | 267 | +24% |
| 500 | 146 | 175 | +20% |
| 1,000 | 85 | 109 | +29% |
| 2,500 | 38.7 | 47.6 | +23% |
| 5,000 | 19.0 | 24.3 | +28% |

The gain stops growing at about 1,000 rows. The binary format only speeds up the cache read. Appwrite still converts each response to JSON before it sends it to the client, and a page of 5,000 rows is 3.4 MB of JSON. That work also grows with the page size, so it limits the gain. Cache memory has no such conversion step, so the memory saving keeps growing.

# Single cached requests are up to 22% faster

![Response time for one cached request of 1,000 and 5,000 rows](/images/blog/faster-list-caching-binary-format/response-time.avif)

With one request at a time, a cached page of 1,000 rows took **32.6 ms** instead of **42.0 ms**. For a page of 5,000 rows, the time went from **217.8 ms** to **175.0 ms**.

# Requests without a TTL are slightly faster

Appwrite also uses Redis to cache documents it reads on almost every request, such as sessions, API keys, and table schemas. These entries now use the binary format too. Requests that don't use a TTL, such as a single `getRow` call or a call for the signed-in account, were about **5%** faster in the benchmark.

# How the benchmark ran

- The test used self-hosted Appwrite 2.3 on a single machine, with a TablesDB table of 10,000 rows. The table had text, enum, array, integer, float, boolean, and datetime columns.
- Both runs used the same server and the same data. The only difference was the cache format.
- The runs alternated between JSON and binary 8 times, with the cache cleared before each run. The tables show the median of 4 runs for each format.
- Each page size got up to 3,000 measured requests after a warm-up, and every request succeeded.

# Availability on Cloud and self-hosted

The binary cache is live on Appwrite Cloud and ships in self-hosted Appwrite 2.3. Self-hosted instances need no migration and no cache flush. After the upgrade, Appwrite reads an old JSON entry as a cache miss, loads the data from the database, and writes the entry again in the binary format. Cache misses rise for a short time after the upgrade.

